1.2GB Chromium Shell.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,517
Look at this raw benchmark comparison, students!

5
00:00:13,517 --> 00:00:16,382
On the left: Microsoft PowerToys Run.

6
00:00:16,382 --> 00:00:18,628
Written in native C++ and C#.

7
00:00:18,628 --> 00:00:28,930
It uses just 35MB of RAM, pops up in 4 milliseconds, and handles math, app launching, and unit conversions with zero cloud telemetry!

8
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:33,481
But look at the right card: The Google App for Windows!

9
00:00:33,481 --> 00:00:40,350
It eats over 1,200MB of RAM—that is literally thirty-four times more memory than PowerToys!

10
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:42,145
Thirty-four times!

11
00:00:42,145 --> 00:00:51,140
If you run that on a 16GB laptop while compiling code or editing video, that 1.2GB bloat causes real system stutter!

12
00:00:51,540 --> 00:01:01,550
But to be fair, James, Google gives you Gemini Flash multimodal vision, live screen translation, and cloud synthesis that PowerToys cannot do on its own!

13
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:03,150
Exactly, Sarah!

14
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:11,789
So the architectural question is: can we get Google's frontier intelligence without paying the 1.2GB memory tax?

15
00:01:11,789 --> 00:01:14,600
That is what we solve in this course!

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,420
That is the fundamental architectural challenge: lean local responsiveness combined with heavy cloud intelligence.

